Swiss law contains no rule against hitchhiking, only against being on foot where the motor traffic is. [https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/1959/679_705_685/de Article 43(3) of the ''Strassenverkehrsgesetz'' (SVG, SR 741.01)] provides that on roads reserved for motor vehicles — ''Autobahnen'' and ''Autostrassen'' — only the vehicle types the Federal Council names may drive, and that "entry is prohibited, access being permitted only at the places provided for it". Going in on foot is therefore out: no thumbing on the carriageway, the hard shoulder or the on-ramp past the green motorway sign, and no walking into a service area from the road behind it. Being dropped at a ''Raststätte'' and asking drivers there is the normal and lawful way to move along the motorway network. On every other road hitchhiking is legal and unregulated.
